<body>
<div class="app">
  <div class="backstrip"><a href="../../subjects.html">← Back to subjects</a></div>
  <div class="topbar">
    <div class="logo"><span class="spark">🌹</span> Romeo and Juliet</div>
    <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div>
    <div class="score">⭐ <span id="score">0</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="spec">Edexcel GCSE English Literature (1ET0) · Romeo and Juliet</div>
  <div class="stage" id="stage">

    <!-- 0 WELCOME -->
    <section class="screen active">
      <span class="tag">Mini-Lesson</span>
      <h1>Romeo and Juliet</h1>
      <p class="big">This mini-lesson drills the <b>knowledge</b> for Shakespeare's <b>Romeo and Juliet</b> (c.1595): the <b>plot</b>, the key <b>characters</b>, the big <b>themes</b> (love, fate, conflict, youth), the <b>Elizabethan context</b> (patriarchy, honour, feuds) and a bank of <b>key quotations and methods</b>.</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 165" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The two feuding houses of Verona">
          <rect x="20" y="45" width="150" height="66" rx="14" fill="#eef6ff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="95" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">Montagues</text>
          <text x="95" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#6b6580">Romeo · Benvolio</text>
          <rect x="390" y="45" width="150" height="66" rx="14" fill="#fdeef0" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="465" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#c8203a">Capulets</text>
          <text x="465" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#6b6580">Juliet · Tybalt</text>
          <text x="280" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#9b5de5">⚔</text>
          <text x="280" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#6b6580">"ancient grudge"</text>
          <text x="280" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">Two households, one feud, in fair Verona</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <p>Work through each screen and answer the <b>questions</b> as you go — plot, character, theme, context and method. Collect ⭐ stars. Press <b>Start</b> when ready.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> these games test the <b>knowledge</b> behind a strong answer — plot, character, theme, context and method. Turning it into an exam essay (thesis, method → effect, structure) is a further skill you build by writing full responses.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1 PLOT -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">The story</span>
      <h2>A tragedy in five acts</h2>
      <p>The <b>Prologue</b> tells us the ending upfront: two "<i>star-cross'd lovers take their life</i>." The plot then unfolds over a few days in <b>Verona</b>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Act 1:</b> The feuding Montagues and Capulets brawl. Lovesick Romeo (over Rosaline) gate-crashes the Capulet ball and meets <b>Juliet</b>; they fall instantly in love, then learn they are enemies.</li>
        <li><b>Act 2:</b> The balcony scene — they declare their love and secretly marry with <b>Friar Lawrence</b>'s help, who hopes it will end the feud.</li>
        <li><b>Act 3:</b> <b>Tybalt</b> kills <b>Mercutio</b>; Romeo avenges him by killing Tybalt and is <b>banished</b>. Juliet's father orders her to marry <b>Paris</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Act 4:</b> Friar Lawrence gives Juliet a potion to fake her death so she can escape to Romeo.</li>
        <li><b>Act 5:</b> The message fails to reach Romeo. Believing Juliet dead, he takes poison; waking to find him dead, Juliet stabs herself. Grief finally <b>ends the feud</b>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 Q: plot (MCQ) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>What does the Prologue reveal?</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>Shakespeare's Prologue calls Romeo and Juliet "<i>star-cross'd lovers</i>." What does this tell the audience?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q1" data-why="'Star-cross'd' means their fate is fixed against them by the stars — the Prologue reveals they will die, so the whole play is coloured by dramatic irony and the theme of fate.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">They will happily marry and end the feud alive. <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Their love is doomed by fate — they are destined to die. <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">They are astronomers who study the stars. <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">They come from the same family. <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 CHARACTERS -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">The people</span>
      <h2>Who's who</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Romeo</b> — young Montague; passionate, impulsive, romantic; moves from moping over Rosaline to devotion to Juliet.</li>
        <li><b>Juliet</b> — young Capulet (not yet 14); grows from obedient daughter to brave, decisive woman who defies her family.</li>
        <li><b>Mercutio</b> — Romeo's witty, hot-headed friend; mocks love (Queen Mab speech); his death ("<i>a plague o' both your houses</i>") turns the play to tragedy.</li>
        <li><b>Tybalt</b> — Juliet's aggressive cousin; embodies the feud and the code of honour; kills Mercutio, is killed by Romeo.</li>
        <li><b>The Nurse</b> — Juliet's earthy, comic confidante; loving but ultimately advises her to abandon Romeo and marry Paris.</li>
        <li><b>Friar Lawrence</b> — well-meaning priest whose secret plans (marriage, potion) go tragically wrong.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 GAME: match character -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Match-up</span>
      <h2>Match each character to their role</h2>
      <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:var(--soft)">Tap a character on the left, then tap the matching description.</p>
      <div class="activity">
        <div id="matchGame"></div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-match"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 Q: character (MCQ) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Whose death changes the play?</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>Whose death in Act 3 turns the play from romantic comedy into tragedy and provokes Romeo's fatal revenge?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q2" data-why="Mercutio is killed by Tybalt in Act 3, cursing 'a plague o' both your houses'. His death is the turning point: Romeo kills Tybalt in revenge and is banished, setting the tragic ending in motion.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Paris <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Mercutio <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The Nurse <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Friar Lawrence <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q2"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 THEMES -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">The big ideas</span>
      <h2>Four themes to master</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl t">Love</span> — passionate, sudden and all-consuming; contrasted with the Nurse's bawdy view and Paris's arranged match. Love and death are entwined.</li>
        <li><span class="hl el">Fate</span> — "<i>star-cross'd</i>", omens and "misadventure" suggest the lovers are doomed from the start.</li>
        <li><span class="hl k">Conflict</span> — the "<i>ancient grudge</i>" between the houses drives the violence; public feud destroys private love.</li>
        <li><span class="hl y">Youth vs age</span> — impulsive young lovers clash with the controlling older generation (Lord Capulet, the Nurse's advice).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Link them:</b> the <b>conflict</b> (feud) and <b>fate</b> trap the young lovers, so their intense <b>love</b> can only end in death — a clash of <b>youth</b> against an unforgiving adult world.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 Q: theme (MCQ) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Why does the feud matter?</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>The Prologue mentions an "<i>ancient grudge</i>" between the two families. Which theme does the feud most drive?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q3" data-why="The 'ancient grudge' is the long-running feud — the theme of conflict. It creates the violence and the divide that makes the lovers' relationship forbidden and doomed.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Comedy and light-heartedness. <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Conflict — the feud between the Montagues and Capulets. <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Religious devotion. <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The joy of youth. <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q3"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 CONTEXT -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Elizabethan context · AO3</span>
      <h2>The world Shakespeare wrote in</h2>
      <p>Written in the <b>1590s</b> for an Elizabethan audience. Context you can weave in:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Patriarchy:</b> fathers controlled daughters and arranged marriages; Juliet defying Lord Capulet over Paris would shock audiences — he calls her "<i>disobedient wretch</i>."</li>
        <li><b>Honour &amp; the code of feuds:</b> male honour demanded revenge for insults; duelling and public brawls (like Tybalt's) were part of this violent code.</li>
        <li><b>Young marriage:</b> girls could marry young, though Juliet at nearly 14 is presented as very young even for the time.</li>
        <li><b>Fate &amp; the heavens:</b> Elizabethans widely believed the stars and fate shaped human lives, so "star-cross'd" felt real, not just poetic.</li>
        <li><b>Religion:</b> a strongly Christian society; Friar Lawrence's role and ideas of sin, marriage and the afterlife carry weight.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

